  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nMonsoon moisture will remain in place at least through the\nupcoming weekend. We have already seem some light showers/virga\nthis morning with an overnight surge of moisture. This will\nlimit thunderstorm coverage through this evening in some areas\ndue to early cloud cover. That said, we do expect an increase in\ncoverage with the better chances behind the current activity\nover the central mountains and higher terrain south of the Snake\nPlain and Magic Valley. We will likely see more isolated storms\nelsewhere, and potential issues with outflow boundaries and\npossible storms along those across the Magic Valley and Snake\nPlain. There is a 30-80% chance for gusts over 35 mph and\npushing 50-60mph with select storms/boundaries through midnight.\nWe do expect a few showers/virga across the central mountains \nand moving into the Magic Valley tonight. There could be a\nlightning strike or two, but the potential is well under 10%.\nMost storms will not produce much if any measurable rain, but\nstronger storms may indeed produce locally heavy downpours and\nsmall hail. We will see the usual uptick tomorrow afternoon and\nevening, with activity more widespread across central and\neastern Idaho. The nature of storms tomorrow will be similar to\ntoday and the past several days. For Friday and Saturday, the\nlatest trends bring a big surge of moisture into Idaho as the\nmonsoon pattern picks up moisture from Tropical Storm Iselle.\nThis is expected to finally clear our area by later Sunday.\nThe current Blend of Models forecast wants to focus higher\nprecipitation totals across the eastern and southeastern\nhighlands, but that may be too confined. We will need to watch\nto see if the deepest moisture is spread farther west, and\nenhanced by thunderstorms. There is moderate to high potential \nfor more than 0.50\" in each 24 hour period, and lower end\npotential for over 1.00\" in each 24 hour period. Afternoon\ntemperatures drops from the 80s and lower 90s to 70s (maybe some\nupper 60s around) during this stretch. \n\nBoth the ECMWF and GFS point toward breezy and drier conditions\nfor the first part of next week. More than one low passes \nthrough to our north, and tries to push the monsoon surge east \nof us. The Blend of Models is not as optimistic and keep some\nchance of showers and storms around.",
